A client's PTO probe as quic-go packs one.

When a probe is due, quic-go first takes the oldest packet still outstanding out of its history and
out of flight and queues its frames to be sent again, and only then packs the probe; if that packs
nothing — what the packet carried was delivered another way — it takes the next oldest, and when none
is left the probe is a PING (QueueProbePacket and sendProbePacket,
vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:1120-1133,
vendor/quic-go/connection.go:2732-2769). Nothing is reported lost: no congestion event, no count, no
ECN verdict. And a PTO in the application data space leaves a packet number out, "in order to elicit
an immediate ACK" (sent_packet_handler.go:989-993).

Upstream ngtcp2 packed new data into a probe when it had any, and went back to an old packet only
when it had none; the old packet stayed in ngtcp2_rtb, in flight, marked reclaimed, so its later
acknowledgement still counted; a client's Initial probe took two packets at once; and no packet
number was left out.

A client now runs ngtcp2_rtb_queue_probe before each probe it packs, in every packet number space,
and leaves a number out on a 1-RTT PTO — the one quic-go's PopPacketNumber hands the timer, and the
scheduled one before it when that is due (:1041-1052). The server keeps upstream's code.

Checked against quic-go's own sentPacketHandler, run by tools/qgovec: test/ngpatch.

